# Code owners are requested for review automatically on every pull request that
# touches a matching path. Branch protection requires their approval before merge.
#
# Later entries win, so the security-sensitive paths below override the catch-all.

* @sirsjg

# Cryptography, the tenant boundary, and the authorization paths. A mistake in
# any of these leaks other people's credentials, so they never merge unreviewed
# even if the rest of the repository is later opened up to more maintainers.
/packages/crypto/          @sirsjg
/packages/auth/            @sirsjg
/packages/authz/           @sirsjg
/packages/tenancy/         @sirsjg
/packages/api-keys/        @sirsjg
/packages/audit/           @sirsjg

# Migrations are checksummed and can never be edited after shipping, and these
# define the row-level-security policies that back the tenant boundary.
/packages/database/migrations/ @sirsjg

# Supply chain and release surface: workflows can exfiltrate secrets, the
# Dockerfile and deploy scripts define what actually runs in production, and
# the lockfiles decide what code gets executed at build time.
/.github/                  @sirsjg
/Dockerfile                @sirsjg
/compose.yaml              @sirsjg
/deploy/                   @sirsjg
/package-lock.json         @sirsjg
/**/go.sum                 @sirsjg

# Security posture documents.
/SECURITY.md               @sirsjg
/docs/security/            @sirsjg
